# Duran Duran pay tribute after death of guitarist Andy Taylor, age 65: “His playing drove the songs that took us around the world”

> 🎵 *Curated Music Story from NME*

Guitarist Andy Taylor of Duran Duran, London, 1981. (Photo by Michael Putland/Getty Images)

Duran Duran have led tributes after the death of guitarist Andy Taylor, who has died aged 65.

Taylor joined the English new wave icons in 1980 during their imperial pop phase through to 1986 when the band were dubbed as ‘The Fab Five’. He was part of the classic line-up reunion from 2001–2006, and would later play as a session musician for the band in 2023 on their last album ‘Danse Macabre’.

Tonight (Wednesday November 25), a joint statement the band and Taylor’s family announced the news that had died “following a long and courageous battle with prostate cancer”. The statement added that he “passed away peacefully at home, surrounded by his family, on Monday November 23, 2026”.

“To the world, Andy was a brilliant guitarist, but to us, he was the most wonderful husband, a loving dad and the proudest grandfather,” shared Taylor’s wife Tracey on behalf of the family. “He was the rock of our family. For all the incredible experiences music brought him, family always came first. Nothing made him happier than having us all together, and no matter how far music took him, his heart was always at home.

“Andy faced the last few years with so much courage and determination. He never gave up, fighting until the very end, never losing his love of life. The kindness and support shown to him by fans from all over the world throughout his illness brought him immense comfort and strength, and for that we will always be grateful.

She added: “Andy knew just how deeply he was loved, and we take great comfort in that. We will miss him every single day.”

Meanwhile, Taylor’s former Duran Duran bandmates honoured his work across classic singles such as ‘Hungry Like The Wolf’, ‘The Wild Boys’, ‘Save A Prayer’, ‘Rio’ and ‘The Reflex’.

“Andy was there at the very beginning of our story,” the band shared. “From the moment he arrived in Birmingham from the North East with a guitar and an unshakeable belief in what we could become, he brought an indelible fire and swagger to our music. His playing drove the songs that took us around the world, and with a dry wit that was matched only by his wonderfully unsentimental, no-nonsense outlook on life he kept us grounded and gave the band its edge through the extraordinary years that followed.

“It was great to make music with him again when the original five reunited in the early 2000s, and that time together, both on stage and in the studio, meant a tremendous amount to all of us. When we were inducted into the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame in 2022, Andy was too unwell to join us, but his words that night reminded everyone of his strength and the bond we shared.”

The band continued: “Andy faced his illness with remarkable courage, honesty and grace. Our thoughts are with his wife Tracey, his children Georgina, Isabella, Bethany and Andrew, and grandson Albie. They all loved him deeply and we ask that their privacy be respected at this very difficult time. In lieu of flowers, the family has asked that donations be made in Andy’s name to Prostate Cancer UK.

“With love from Simon, John, Nick and Roger.”

The remaining Duran Duran members also shared individual statements on their website, with frontman Simon Le Bon remembering “the blond bombshell, firebrand teenager I met in 1980″.

“He’s the kid from the Northeast who can really play guitar,” he recalled. “If you like, he can play it like Mick Ronson, or he can play it like Jimmy Page, he can even play rinky-dink rhythm like Nile Rodgers. But what he does most is play guitar like guitar-genius – Andy Taylor! He came fresh off touring Army bases in West Germany. Spikey hair and spikey personality. Andy didn’t mince his words; he didn’t suffer fools gladly. Andy never took the easy musical route. His overdrive, overdrove us into being the only funk/rock/punk band in the New Romantic movement, with songs that still survive, decades later when his own life is cut so short.”

Le Bon went on: “Me and him had a special bond right from the start. He’s the one who gave me my band name – ‘Charley’. And after 2006, when he wasn’t touring with us, we spent happy days at the studio in his Ibiza villa, laying down tracks for our latest records. I have so many wonderful, hilarious, tragic memories of our time together: on tour, in the studio, in our little flat on Caroline Road, Balsall Heath at 6am. And through it, and above it all, he was in love with his family – most important thing in his life.

“There’s a massive Andy Taylor shaped hole in my heart. It’ll never close up; I won’t let it. I will miss him and love him for all my days.”

Bassist John Taylor meanwhile, hailed the guitar as “a demon” as well as “a killer riff-merchant, melodist, six string textualist (he would hate that term) and a singer with a great voice.”

Keyboardist Nick Rhodes described Taylor as “sharp as a razor and blunt as a stone” with “blood like electricity, with a current that flowed from his head through his fingertips” while now former drummer Roger Taylor added: “The ‘Geordie Genius’, ‘the live wire from Newcastle’: how fortunate we were to have found him.”

Born in Tynemouth in Northumberland in 1961 before growing up in Cullercoats, Taylor’s family worked in the local fishing industry. He began playing guitar age 11 when he was tutored by Dave Black from Bowie’s Spiders From Mars. After leaving school early, he would join Duran Duran in 1980 after they advertised for a guitarist in Melody Maker.

Taylor was also a member of The Power Station with Robert Palmer, released four solo albums under his own name – the last being 2024’s self-titled – and worked as guitarist, songwriter, and record producer with the likes of Rod Stewart, Mark Shaw and Belinda Carlisle. He published his memoir Wild Boy: My Life In Duran Duran in 2008

He is survived by his wife Tracey Wilson, and their four children Andrew, Georgina, Bethany and Isabella.

The news comes after Roger Taylor announced earlier this month that he’d be “stepping down from the band and retiring from playing the drums” due to health issues.

